\smjauxentry{art:divisors}{title}{A Classification of Principal Divisors on $\PazoBB  {CP}^1$}
\smjauxentry{art:divisors}{author}{Pablo Santos Guerrero}
\smjauxentry{art:divisors}{year}{MMDA'27}
\smjauxentry{art:divisors}{affil}{UgS V}
\smjauxentry{art:divisors}{category}{article}
\smjauxentry{art:divisors}{cover-title}{A Classification of Principal Divisors on $\PazoBB  {CP}^1$}
\smjauxentry{art:divisors}{toc-title}{A Classification of Principal Divisors on $\PazoBB  {CP}^1$}
\smjauxentry{art:divisors}{no-header}{}
\smjauxentry{art:divisors}{abstract}{Every nonzero polynomial over $\PazoBB  {C}$ has exactly as many roots as its degree. This paper develops the analogue of that statement for the much larger class of meromorphic functions on the complex projective line $\PazoBB  {CP}^1 = \PazoBB  {C} \cup \{\infty \}$, where a function may not only vanish but also blow up. To each such function we attach its divisor: the formal record of where it vanishes and where it has poles, together with the integer multiplicity at each such point. Divisors form an abelian group $\operatorname  {Div}(\PazoBB  {CP}^1)$; those arising from functions form a subgroup $\operatorname  {Prin}(\PazoBB  {CP}^1)$; and the total multiplicity gives a homomorphism $\deg : \operatorname  {Div}(\PazoBB  {CP}^1) \to \PazoBB  {Z}$. Our main result is that on $\PazoBB  {CP}^1$ these two notions agree exactly: $\operatorname  {Prin}(\PazoBB  {CP}^1) = \ker (\deg )$. From this we compute the divisor class group $\operatorname  {Cl}(\PazoBB  {CP}^1) \cong  \PazoBB  {Z}$ and, by an explicit construction with transition functions, the Picard group $\operatorname  {Pic}(\PazoBB  {CP}^1) \cong  \PazoBB  {Z}$.}
\newlabel{art:divisors}{{8}{59}{}{section*.26}{}}
\newlabel{art:divisors@cref}{{}{[1][59][]59}{}{}{}}
